//? Title: Building Computational Graphs: Names, Sharing, Order and Loops
//? Subtitle: Static graphs against define-by-run tapes, parameter names and weight sharing, control dependencies, unrolled and data-dependent loops, derived, checked by hand and applied to a tied-weight autoencoder of handwritten digits, using datamunge's nn::ParameterStore and nn::tape
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
TensorFlow 1 built a model as a graph first and ran it later. `datamunge::nn` runs each operation as it is called and records a
tape only for gradients. This report shows what each graph-building concept becomes in eager code, and checks the mathematics
behind each. Scoped op names become parameter paths in a store, where asking for a name again means *sharing* the parameter.
A shared parameter's gradient is the sum of the gradients of its uses, which the theory derives and the
checks confirm to rounding error. Control dependencies become program order: the counter reads are 0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 3, 3, as in
TensorFlow. Construction errors are raised by the faulty call and leave later calls unaffected. The tape is always in topological
order. An unrolled loop of \(T\) steps records exactly \(3T+1\) operations, and a guarded one records none. A data-dependent Newton iteration for
\(\sqrt a\) records 17 to 44 operations depending on \(a\), and differentiating through it gives \(1/(2\sqrt a)\) to within \(2\times10^{-16}\).
The derivative converges as fast as the iterate, because the iteration map is flat at its fixed point. On the digits data, a linear
autoencoder whose encoder and decoder share one weight matrix reaches the PCA optimum, with
orthonormal columns and the PCA subspace to \(10^{-3}\), using half the parameters of an untied one.
)

\toc

> The problem

A neural network is a composition of operations, and both training and inspection need that composition as data:
to differentiate it, to name and save its parameters, to share parameters between parts, and to reason about the order of
side effects. TensorFlow 1 made the graph explicit. A `tensorflow::Scope` named each op ("encoder/layer/Add_1"), placed it on a device,
attached control dependencies, and collected construction errors. A `Session` then ran parts of the finished graph. Eager libraries such as PyTorch and
`datamunge::nn` instead run each operation immediately ("define by run"). The only graph is the autograd tape that tensors requiring gradients record.

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.1_tensors_graphs_sessions/graph_construction.cpp`. For each
TensorFlow concept it says what plays the same role in `nn`, and it checks the mathematics behind it: the multivariate chain rule for shared
parameters, the ordering of side effects, the size of the graph of a loop, and differentiation through data-dependent control flow.

> The method

>> Graphs, static and dynamic

A computational graph is a directed acyclic graph whose nodes are operations and whose edges carry tensors. Evaluating it
requires a *topological order*, in which each node comes after all of its inputs. Differentiating it requires the reverse order.
A *static* graph is built once and can be inspected, optimised (common subexpressions merged, constants folded) and placed on
devices before it runs. A *dynamic* graph is the trace of one execution. Ordinary C++ control flow (`if`, `for`, `while`) decides which
operations run, so the graph may differ from call to call. `nn::tape(outputs)` returns that trace, listed from the output back to the leaves.
In that order every node precedes the nodes its inputs came from.

>> Names and scopes

In TensorFlow every op has a unique hierarchical name, and a repeated name is made unique ("block", "block_1"). In eager code only
*parameters* need stable names, for saving, loading, regularising and sharing. `nn::ParameterStore::get(name, shape, init)` creates a
parameter on first use and returns the same tensor on every later call. Prefixes such as "encoder/" play the role of scopes. A repeated
name therefore means the same parameter, not a new one. That is how layers share weights.

>> The gradient of a shared parameter

Suppose a parameter \(\theta\) is used in \(m\) places of a computation \(L=F(\theta,\theta,\dots,\theta)\). Write \(\theta^{(1)},\dots,\theta^{(m)}\) for the uses,
as if they were separate copies, and set all of them equal to \(\theta\). By the multivariate chain rule,

$$
\frac{\mathrm dL}{\mathrm d\theta}=\sum_{j=1}^m\frac{\partial F}{\partial\theta^{(j)}}\Big|_{\theta^{(1)}=\dots=\theta^{(m)}=\theta} .
$$
\alttext(The total derivative of L with respect to theta is the sum over the uses j from 1 to m of the partial derivative of F with respect to the j-th copy of theta, evaluated with all copies equal to theta.)

A tape implements this sum automatically, because each use is an edge into the same leaf, and the leaf's accumulator adds every
adjoint that reaches it. A tied autoencoder that encodes with \(W\) and decodes with \(W^\top\) gets the encoder's gradient plus the decoder's gradient.

>> Control dependencies and side effects

In a static graph, operations without a data path between them may run in any order. A control dependency forces "run \(a\) before \(b\)" when
\(a\) has a side effect, such as an assignment, that \(b\) must observe. In eager code statements run in program order. A control dependency is
simply a statement written earlier, and its absence is a statement left out. Side effects on tensors (in-place updates such as `add_`) take
effect immediately.

>> Errors

A TensorFlow `Scope` keeps the first construction error, skips every later op, and refuses to serialise the graph. In eager code there is
nothing to poison. The faulty call throws an exception that names the shapes involved, and the next call runs normally.

>> Loops: unrolled and data-dependent

A loop in eager code records one copy of its body per iteration. A recurrence \(h_{s+1}=\tanh(Uh_s+x)\) run for \(T\) steps records \(T\) matrix products,
\(T\) additions and \(T\) tanh operations, so the tape and the memory of its saved tensors grow linearly in \(T\). This is backpropagation through time.
Under `nn::NoGradGuard` nothing is recorded.

A loop whose trip count depends on data is no harder: the tape records the iterations that ran. Newton's method for \(\sqrt a\),
\(r_{k+1}=\Phi(r_k,a)=\tfrac12(r_k+a/r_k)\), stopped once \(\lvert r^2-a\rvert\le10^{-12}a\), is differentiable through the loop. The derivative of the iterate obeys

$$
\frac{\mathrm dr_{k+1}}{\mathrm da}=\Phi_r(r_k,a)\frac{\mathrm dr_k}{\mathrm da}+\Phi_a(r_k,a),\qquad\Phi_r=\tfrac12\Bigl(1-\frac{a}{r_k^2}\Bigr),\quad\Phi_a=\frac1{2r_k} .
$$
\alttext(The derivative of r k plus 1 with respect to a equals Phi r at r k and a times the derivative of r k, plus Phi a at r k and a, where Phi r is one half of 1 minus a over r k squared, and Phi a is 1 over 2 r k.)

At the fixed point \(r^*=\sqrt a\), \(\Phi_r=0\), so the recursion forgets \(\mathrm dr_k/\mathrm da\) and gives \(\Phi_a(r^*,a)=1/(2\sqrt a)\). That is the implicit-function
derivative \(\Phi_a/(1-\Phi_r)\). Near the fixed point \(\Phi_r\) is proportional to the error \(r_k-\sqrt a\), so the derivative's error is of the order of the
iterate's error: it converges quadratically too. For an iteration with \(\Phi_r\ne0\) at its fixed point, the unrolled derivative would converge only linearly.

> Simple examples

>> Names, sharing, a tape, order and errors

The program creates three parameters ("a", "encoder/w" and "block/k", the last asked for twice) and computes
\(y=\sum\bigl[(h_2k)\cdot\mathbf 1_{1\times2}\bigr]w\) with \(h_2=a+2+3\) and \(w=I\). It then prints the tape and the gradients, runs the counter of TensorFlow's
control-dependency example, and triggers a shape error.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  // Names: parameters live in a store under path-like names; prefixes play the role of scopes.
  nn::ParameterStore store(1);
  Tensor a = store.get("a", {}, nn::init::constant(1));
  Tensor w = store.get("encoder/w", {2, 2}, [](const nn::Shape&, nn::DType d, nn::Generator&) { return nn::tensor({{1, 0}, {0, 1}}, d); });
  const Tensor b = nn::scalar(2), c = nn::scalar(3);  // constants: no name, no history
  const Tensor h1 = a + b, h2 = h1 + c;
  // Asking for an existing name returns the same tensor: reuse, not a new "block_1".
  Tensor p = store.get("block/k", {}, nn::init::constant(5));
  Tensor q = store.get("block/k", {}, nn::init::constant(7));  // initialiser ignored
  std::print("- parameters: {}\n", store.names());
  std::print("- store.get(\"block/k\") twice: same tensor = {}, value {}\n", p.is_same(q), q.item());
  std::print("- h1 = a + b records `{}`, h2 = h1 + c records `{}`, h2 = {}\n\n", h1.grad_fn_name(), h2.grad_fn_name(), h2.item());

  const Tensor y = nn::sum(nn::matmul(nn::reshape(h2 * p, {1, 1}) * nn::ones({1, 2}), w));
  std::vector<std::pair<Tensor, std::string>> names;
  for (const auto& n : store.names()) names.emplace_back(store.at(n), n);
  const auto t = nn::tape({y}, names);
  std::print("```\n{}```\n\n", t.to_text());
  const auto g = nn::grad(y, {a, p, w});
  std::print("- y = {}; dy/da = {}, dy/d(block/k) = {}, dy/dw = {}\n\n", y.item(), g[0].item(), g[1].item(), g[2].values_str());

  // Control dependencies are program order: a read placed after an increment sees it.
  Tensor counter = nn::scalar(0, nn::DType::Int32);
  auto inc = [&] { counter.add_(nn::scalar(1, nn::DType::Int32)); };
  std::vector<int> reads;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) reads.push_back(counter.item<std::int32_t>());
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) inc(), reads.push_back(counter.item<std::int32_t>());
  reads.push_back(counter.item<std::int32_t>());
  std::print("- counter reads: three plain, three after an increment, one plain: {}\n", reads);

  // Errors are raised by the call that causes them; later calls are unaffected.
  const Tensor m = nn::tensor({{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}});
  try {
    (void)nn::matmul(m, m);
  } catch (const std::exception& e) {
    std::print("- matmul([2, 3], [2, 3]): `{}`\n", e.what());
  }
  std::print("- the next call runs normally: neg(m) = {}\n", nn::neg(m).values_str());

}
```
