// result_end

\caption(Left: operations on the tape of an unrolled tanh recurrence against the number of steps \(T\), on a straight line \(3T+1\). Right: for Newton's method on \(\sqrt2\) from \(r_0=1\), the error of the iterate (black) and of its derivative with respect to \(a\) computed through the loop (red), on a log scale. Errors that are exactly zero are drawn at \(10^{-17}\).)
\alttext(Left: seven blue points on a straight line from 4 operations at T equals 1 to 193 at T equals 64. Right: two curves over k from 1 to 7. The black value error falls from about 0.09 to 0.0025, 2 times 10 to the minus 6 and 1.6 times 10 to the minus 12, then levels at 2 times 10 to the minus 16. The red derivative error falls alongside it, slightly above, from 0.15 to 1.8 times 10 to the minus 11 at k equals 4, and is exactly zero from k equals 5.)

All four predictions hold.

- The tied gradient equals the sum of the encoder-copy and decoder-copy gradients exactly: the tape adds the same two products the copies compute.
- Every node of the tape is listed before its inputs.
- The recurrence records exactly three operations per step plus the final sum, from 4 operations at \(T=1\) to 193 at \(T=64\), and nothing under the guard.
- The Newton loop ran 5 to 14 times depending on \(a\), so its tape had 17 to 44 operations: 2 for the start, then 3 per iteration. The derivative through it
  matched \(1/(2\sqrt a)\) to every printed digit, and to \(2\times10^{-16}\) at \(a=10^6\).

For \(a=2\), the right panel shows the derivative error falling with the iterate's error: \(0.15,\;7.6\times10^{-3},\;1.3\times10^{-5},\;1.8\times10^{-11}\). Each is about
the square of the previous one, as the analysis predicts, and it is exactly zero from \(k=5\). Differentiating a converged solver by unrolling it gave the implicit-function answer
here, because \(\Phi_r(r^*)=0\).

> A dataset: a tied-weight autoencoder for handwritten digits

The digits data hold 1797 images of \(8\times8\) pixels with intensities 0 to 16. They are scaled to \([0,1]\) and centred, giving a \(1797\times64\) matrix \(X\).
A linear autoencoder compresses each image to \(k\) numbers and reconstructs it. The *tied* version uses one parameter, `autoencoder/W`
(\(64\times k\)), both to encode, \(h=XW\), and to decode, \(\hat X=hW^\top\). The *untied* version has separate `encoder/W` and `decoder/W`. Both minimise the mean squared
reconstruction error with Adam (step 0.01, 4000 steps), from small orthogonal starting weights.

For a linear autoencoder the answer is known (Baldi and Hornik, 1989). The best rank-\(k\) reconstruction projects onto the span of the top \(k\)
principal components, with mean squared error per pixel equal to the sum of the discarded eigenvalues of \(X^\top X/n\) divided by 64. For the tied model,
\(\hat X=XWW^\top\) is a projection only if \(W^\top W=I\), so at the optimum the columns of \(W\) are orthonormal and span the principal subspace. The untied
optimum needs only \(DE\) to be that projection, so \(E\) on its own has no reason to be orthonormal.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/graph_construction_autoencoder.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;
  // 1797 digits, 64 pixels scaled to [0, 1] and centred.
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t n = 1797, d = 64;
  std::vector<double> v(static_cast<std::size_t>(n * d));
  for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < d; ++j) {
    const auto c = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
    const double m = std::accumulate(c.begin(), c.end(), 0.0) / n / 16.0;
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) v[i * d + j] = c[i] / 16.0 - m;
  }
  const auto label = digits.get_column<double>("digit").values();
  const Tensor X = nn::from_vector(v, {n, d});
  // PCA from the eigen-decomposition of the covariance X^T X / n (eigenvalues ascending).
  const auto eig = nn::eigh(nn::matmul(nn::transpose(X), X) / static_cast<double>(n));
  const auto lambda = eig.e.to_vector<double>();
  const double total = std::accumulate(lambda.begin(), lambda.end(), 0.0);

  std::print("| k | tied: loss | untied: loss | PCA optimum | tied: largest entry of WᵀW − I | untied: largest entry of EᵀE − I | tied: subspace distance to PCA | parameters tied / untied |\n");
  std::print("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> curves;
  Tensor codes2;
  for (std::int64_t k : {2, 5, 10}) {
    double optimum = 0;  // mean squared error per entry = sum of the discarded eigenvalues / d
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < d - k; ++j) optimum += lambda[j];
    optimum /= d;
    // Tied: one parameter, used as the encoder W and (transposed) as the decoder.
    nn::ParameterStore tied_store(7, F64);
    const Tensor W = tied_store.get("autoencoder/W", {d, k}, nn::init::orthogonal(0.1));
    nn::Optimizer tied_opt(tied_store, 0.01, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
    // Untied: separate encoder and decoder.
    nn::ParameterStore untied_store(7, F64);
    const Tensor E = untied_store.get("encoder/W", {d, k}, nn::init::orthogonal(0.1));
    const Tensor D = untied_store.get("decoder/W", {k, d}, nn::init::orthogonal(0.1));
    nn::Optimizer untied_opt(untied_store, 0.01, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
    std::vector<double> curve;
    double lt = 0, lu = 0;
    for (int step = 0; step < 4000; ++step) {
      const Tensor tied_loss = nn::mean(nn::square(nn::matmul(nn::matmul(X, W), nn::transpose(W)) - X));
      tied_opt.step(nn::grad(tied_loss, {W}));
      const Tensor untied_loss = nn::mean(nn::square(nn::matmul(nn::matmul(X, E), D) - X));
      untied_opt.step(nn::grad(untied_loss, {E, D}));
      lt = tied_loss.item(), lu = untied_loss.item();
      if (k == 5) curve.push_back(std::log10(lt / optimum - 1 + 1e-12)), curve.push_back(std::log10(lu / optimum - 1 + 1e-12));
    }
    if (k == 5) curves.push_back(curve);
    nn::NoGradGuard guard;
    const Tensor WtW = nn::matmul(nn::transpose(W), W);
    const double orth = nn::max(nn::abs(WtW - nn::eye(k, F64))).item();
    const double orth_e = nn::max(nn::abs(nn::matmul(nn::transpose(E), E) - nn::eye(k, F64))).item();
    // Projector onto span(W) against the projector onto the top-k eigenvectors.
    const Tensor P = nn::matmul(W, nn::solve(WtW, nn::transpose(W)));
    const Tensor V = nn::slice(eig.v, {0, d - k}, {d, k});
    const double dist = nn::max(nn::abs(P - nn::matmul(V, nn::transpose(V)))).item();
    std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.1e} | {:.2f} | {:.1e} | {} / {} |\n", k, lt, lu, optimum, orth, orth_e, dist, d * k, 2 * d * k);
    if (k == 2) codes2 = nn::matmul(X, W);
  }
  std::print("\nTotal variance per pixel {:.5f}; the top 2, 5 and 10 components keep {:.1f}%, {:.1f}% and {:.1f}% of it.\n", total / d,
             100 * (lambda[63] + lambda[62]) / total,
             100 * std::accumulate(lambda.end() - 5, lambda.end(), 0.0) / total,
             100 * std::accumulate(lambda.end() - 10, lambda.end(), 0.0) / total);

  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> steps, ct, cu;
  for (std::size_t s = 0; s < curves[0].size() / 2; ++s) {
    steps.push_back(static_cast<double>(s));
    ct.push_back(std::clamp(curves[0][2 * s], -8.0, 2.0));
    cu.push_back(std::clamp(curves[0][2 * s + 1], -8.0, 2.0));
  }
  left.lines(steps, cu, "untied", dm::graphics::RGB{234, 140, 0}, 1.5);
  left.lines(steps, ct, "tied", dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 1.5);
  left.x_limits(0, 4000).y_limits(-8, 2).title("k = 5: excess loss over PCA").x_label("Adam step").y_label("log10 (loss / optimum − 1)");
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const auto cv = codes2.to_vector<double>();
  // A qualitative palette, half transparent so the digit labels stay readable.
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> pal{{31, 119, 180, 0.45}, {255, 127, 14, 0.45}, {44, 160, 44, 0.45}, {214, 39, 40, 0.45},
                                           {148, 103, 189, 0.45}, {140, 86, 75, 0.45}, {227, 119, 194, 0.45}, {127, 127, 127, 0.45},
                                           {188, 189, 34, 0.45}, {23, 190, 207, 0.45}};
  for (int digit = 0; digit < 10; ++digit) {
    std::vector<double> cx, cy;
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
      if (label[i] == digit) cx.push_back(cv[2 * i]), cy.push_back(cv[2 * i + 1]);
    right.points(cx, cy, "", pal[digit], 2.5);
  }
  for (int digit = 0; digit < 10; ++digit) {
    double sx = 0, sy = 0, m = 0;
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
      if (label[i] == digit) sx += cv[2 * i], sy += cv[2 * i + 1], ++m;
    right.text(sx / m, sy / m, std::to_string(digit), dm::graphics::RGB{0, 0, 0}, 22.0);
  }
  double lo = 0, hi = 0;
  for (double e : cv) lo = std::min(lo, e), hi = std::max(hi, e);
  right.x_limits(lo - 0.1, hi + 0.1).y_limits(lo - 0.1, hi + 0.1).title("k = 2: codes X W, by digit").x_label("code 1").y_label("code 2");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(left);
  fig.add(right);
  fig.size(1100, 480).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/graph_construction_autoencoder.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| k | tied: loss | untied: loss | PCA optimum | tied: largest entry of WᵀW − I | untied: largest entry of EᵀE − I | tied: subspace distance to PCA | parameters tied / untied |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | 0.052426 | 0.052426 | 0.052426 | 4.7e-05 | 0.10 | 4.0e-04 | 128 / 256 |
| 5 | 0.033369 | 0.033369 | 0.033369 | 9.1e-05 | 0.09 | 4.8e-04 | 320 / 640 |
| 10 | 0.019197 | 0.019197 | 0.019196 | 2.0e-04 | 0.13 | 7.8e-04 | 640 / 1280 |

Total variance per pixel 0.07333; the top 2, 5 and 10 components keep 28.5%, 54.5% and 73.8% of it.
\image(figures/graph_construction_autoencoder.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: excess loss of the \(k=5\) autoencoders over the PCA optimum during training, on a log scale; values below \(10^{-8}\) are drawn at \(10^{-8}\). Right: the two-number codes \(XW\) of the tied \(k=2\) model, one colour per digit, with each digit's mean code labelled.)
\alttext(Left: two noisy curves over 4000 steps, orange for untied and blue for tied, both starting at 0 and falling below minus 5 within about 300 steps, then fluctuating between about minus 8 and minus 5. Right: a cloud of 1797 semi-transparent points over codes from about minus 1.8 to 1.9. Digit 0 is at the upper left near (minus 1.1, 0.8), 6 at the left near (minus 1.2, minus 0.3), 4 at the lower left, 9 and 3 at the top, 2 at the right, 1 and 7 at the lower right, and 5 and 8 in the centre, overlapping.)

Both models reach the PCA optimum, to six decimals for \(k=2\) and 5 and to one unit in the sixth for \(k=10\). The tied model gets there with half the
parameters (128 against 256 for \(k=2\)), and, as the theory requires, its \(W^\top W\) is the identity to \(2\times10^{-4}\). Its projector agrees with the projector onto the top \(k\)
eigenvectors to \(8\times10^{-4}\) in every entry. The untied encoder's \(E^\top E\) is 0.09 to 0.13 away from the identity: the same loss, reached with a non-orthonormal
encoder. The remaining excess loss, \(10^{-8}\) to \(10^{-5}\) relative, is Adam's noise at a constant step, not a different solution. Two numbers keep 28.5% of the pixel
variance, and they already separate 0, 6, 4, 3 and 2 from each other, while 5 and 8 overlap in the middle. Ten numbers keep 73.8%. The sharing itself needed nothing
but asking the store for one name and using the tensor twice. The tape summed the encoder's and decoder's contributions, as the first check confirmed.

> Conclusion

In eager code a computational graph is a by-product: the tape that one execution records for differentiation. Every static-graph concept
has a simpler counterpart. Op names become parameter paths. Scopes become prefixes. Reuse by name becomes weight sharing, with gradients summed over
uses by the multivariate chain rule. Control dependencies become statement order, and the construction errors of a sticky scope become exceptions at the faulty call.
Dynamic graphs make loops and data-dependent control flow ordinary C++. The price is that the tape grows with every iteration, linearly for an unrolled
recurrence, and that nothing can be optimised before it runs. A loop that need not be differentiated should run under `NoGradGuard`.

The checks confirmed each piece exactly where exact answers exist. A tied gradient equalled the sum of its parts. A loop of \(T\) steps recorded \(3T+1\) operations.
Differentiating through a converged Newton solver gave the implicit-function derivative, with the derivative converging quadratically because the
iteration is flat at its fixed point. On the digits data, sharing one matrix between encoder and decoder halved the parameters, reached the PCA optimum,
and forced the orthonormal columns that the untied model lacked.

